{
 "id": "l26",
 "label": "26",
 "title": "The preposition sa",
 "summary": "One small word for in, to, from, for, on, into, over, through and at; position words; gáling, mulâ, búhat and hanggáng",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "See how sa does the work of many English prepositions",
  "Use sa with position words: sa loób, sa labás, sa itaás…",
  "Use sa may for near or about",
  "Say where someone comes from with gáling sa, mulâ sa and búhat sa",
  "Talk about periods of time with mulâ, búhat and hanggáng"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "One word, many prepositions",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A **preposition** is a small word that links a phrase to the rest of a sentence, such as *in*, *to*, *from*, *into*, *on*, *for*, *through* and *at*. English has many of them. Tagalog has one very flexible word, **[[sa|marker]]**, that does most of their jobs."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "You met **sa** in Lesson 6 (in, at, on, to) and Lesson 25 (sa + place answers a saán question). This lesson shows all the English words it can stand for."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "English",
      "Book example with sa"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "in",
       "Nag-áaral kamí **sa** silíd-aralán."
      ],
      [
       "to",
       "Pupuntá kamí **sa** Tagaytay búkas."
      ],
      [
       "from",
       "Gáling kamí **sa** Baguio."
      ],
      [
       "for",
       "Ang damít na itó ay para **sa** kanyá."
      ],
      [
       "on",
       "Ang aklát **sa** mesa ay bago."
      ],
      [
       "into",
       "Tumalón ang aso **sa** ilog."
      ],
      [
       "over",
       "Aawit siya **sa** radyo."
      ],
      [
       "through",
       "Nagdaán siyá **sa** bintanà."
      ],
      [
       "at",
       "Nagsúsulát ako **sa** gabí."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "The main uses of sa"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "Think of it this way",
     "t": "When you translate into English, choose the preposition that fits the sentence. When you speak Tagalog, you only need **sa**."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "culture",
     "title": "Why Filipinos mix up English prepositions",
     "t": "Because sa is used for all of these in Tagalog and other Philippine languages, the book suggests this may be why Filipinos find the many English prepositions hard to learn."
    }
   ]
  },
