  {
   "title": "Practice from the book",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The book's exercise is a short story about a trip to Tagaytay City, written in English. You translate it into Tagalog, then underline every phrase with **sa**. The book prints no answer, so use it as free practice."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "The story (in brief)",
     "items": [
      "A group goes to Tagaytay for a picnic; the trip (**biyahe**) from Manila takes 45 minutes by car.",
      "They buy pineapples, bananas and oranges, see smoke on top of Taal volcano, and walk down to Taal Lake.",
      "The scenery (**tánáwin**) is beautiful; some sit on the grass (**damó**).",
      "They drink juice from young coconuts (**tubig ng niyóg**) at Taal Vista Lodge and go home to Manila at four in the afternoon."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "trip",
     "title": "Vocabulary for the story"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Remember",
     "items": [
      "Sa can mean in, to, from, for, on, into, over, through or at.",
      "Sa + position word: sa loób, sa labás, sa haráp, sa tabí, sa itaás, sa gitnâ, sa pagítan, sa likurán, sa ibabâ.",
      "Sa becomes nása when answering násaán. Sa may = near or about.",
      "Gáling sa, mulâ sa, búhat sa = from. For a hometown, only mulâ sa or búhat sa.",
      "For time: mulâ / búhat (from) and hanggáng (until). Native speakers often drop sa."
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
